Football is back!🎉 And my Chicago Bears 🐻⬇️🏈 came out swinging 💥🚀 

The [nflverse](https://nflverse.nflverse.com/) data I'm excited to work with generally goes back to 1999... but this was the most the Bears have scored since 1980. So. We're out of sample, and I'm okay with that. 

But we still had some issues: Wow, the penalties, and defense was shaky, particularly the corners. But that run game was 🔥, hoping we can build on the much improved offensive line from last year.


# Run direction

The Bear's offensive line was finally a bright spot last year; left tackle has been an ongoing struggle, but left guard is the best (Joe Thuney), and the right side with Jonah Jackson and especially our monster right tackle Darnell Wright is a huge strength. (Side note: Wright an amazing athlete and we need to see him on the receiving end of a pass, preferably a touchdown pass, before the end of the season.) So the Bears go right because of Wright. 

EMBED GRUDEN

Let's check out last year, and see if we continued it week 1 of 2026. 

The [nflverse](https://nflverse.nflverse.com/) play-by-play data records run location (left, middle, right) and for left and right an additional note of gap (between the A and B gap as guard, C gap as tackle, and D gap as end).



right 66% of the time (gruden)



I am interested in play calling and the direction a play is designed to run, so I am focusing on plays that seem to be intended as rushes (i.e., not quarterback scrambles), and we can interpret the direction (i.e., we may not know to intended location for rush attempts when there was a fumble in the backfield). 


::: {.callout-note collapse="true"}
## Nitty-gritty details of rush attempts that are excluded

This is the real work of getting familiar with data like this (but the less interesting story to tell), getting into the messy details of what plays actually have location and gap information that I can use, what is missing it that seems like it should have it, and what I may want to conceptually exclude. 

I will *exclude*: quarterback scrambles (they were intended as passes, and the direction they go is often in reaction to a breakdown rather than a plan), quarterback kneel downs (this isn't really a play, and doesn't have a location and gap coded anyways), fumbles when there is no run location (that is, run location is NA; a lot of these were fumbles by the quarterback, most result in no gain, small numbers seem to be chaotic recoveries with some gain, but overall it seems we can't interpret a rush direction plan with the quarterback fumble). In this data, two point attempts do not have location information, so I'll have to drop those as well. 

Finally, I also removed 6 plays with penalties for illegal forward passes as these were likely not intended to be running plays. (This was a somewhat incidental find as 2 of these plays had left/right location but lacked gap, and upon further reflection I decided I did not want any of these plays.)

I did not remove any other plays for penalties.

I will note that (other than the fumbles) even when a run did not gain positive yards, the data has coded for the (presumably intended) location and gap. And fumbles with yards gained also had locations (seemingly, these would mostly be fumbles later down the field).
:::




Because I'm primarily interested in my Chicago Bears, I'm going to look at the NFL *when excluding Chicago*, so the **rest of the NFL**. And the rest of the NFL is pretty balanced in their split between left and right, and picking either direction a bit more often than the middle (which makes sense given that each direction has multiple gaps to choose from) across both the 2025 regular season and in the opening week 1 of 2026.



